title: A tiny calculator: the ALU
mode: draw
chapter: Choosing
check: behaves
parts: and or not chip:my_xor chip:my_mux4
needs: my_xor my_mux my_mux4
---- intro
The heart of a processor is the **ALU** (arithmetic logic unit): it gets two inputs and an **operation** number, works out every answer at once, and a multiplexer picks the one asked for. Here, for one bit:

| op1 | op0 | y |
|-----|-----|---|
| 0 | 0 | `a` AND `b` |
| 0 | 1 | `a` OR `b` |
| 1 | 0 | `a` XOR `b` |
| 1 | 1 | NOT `a` |

Your **my_mux4** from the last lesson picks one of four, with `op1 op0` as its `s1 s0`.
---- task
Build the one-bit ALU: `y` is the result of the operation `op1 op0` picks.
---- start
input a 0 0
input b 0 70
input op0 0 300
input op1 0 360
led y 520 160
---- reference
input a, b, op0, op1
led y
r0 = a & b
r1 = a | b
r2 = a ^ b
r3 = ~a
lo = (r0 & ~op0) | (r1 & op0)
hi = (r2 & ~op0) | (r3 & op0)
y = (lo & ~op1) | (hi & op1)
---- answer
input a 0 0
input b 0 70
input op0 0 300
input op1 0 360
and g1 160 0
or o1 160 80
chip x1 my_xor 160 160
not n1 160 240
chip m1 my_mux4 340 150
led y 520 160
a -> g1.0
b -> g1.1
a -> o1.0
b -> o1.1
a -> x1.a
b -> x1.b
a -> n1.in
g1 -> m1.d0
o1 -> m1.d1
x1.y -> m1.d2
n1 -> m1.d3
op0 -> m1.s0
op1 -> m1.s1
m1.y -> y
---- hint1
Make all four answers: an AND, an OR, a my_xor and a NOT, each fed by `a` (and `b`).
---- hint2
The four answers go into my_mux4's `d0` to `d3` in the order of the table; `op0` and `op1` go into `s0` and `s1`.
---- solution
Four parts compute AND, OR, XOR and NOT of the inputs all at once. my_mux4, steered by `op1 op0`, passes the one asked for to `y`.
---- done
Every instruction a processor runs, add, and, or, compare, is the ALU computing all of its answers and a multiplexer keeping one. Make it 32 bits wide and add an adder, and it is the real thing.
